/T (In a typical Node.js application, there is a single process that handles all incoming requests. This process runs on a single CPU core and can become a bottleneck as the number of requests increases. With clustering, you can create multiple worker processes that can handle requests in parallel, spreading the load across multiple CPU cores.)
